{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mass.BranchLocusFiniteOrder","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mass.BranchLocusFiniteOrder","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Complex.Basic"],"declarations":5,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.BranchLocusFiniteOrder.not_stabilizes_of_one_lt_complex","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.BranchLocusFiniteOrder.not_stabilizes_of_one_lt_complex","kind":"theorem","line":150,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/BranchLocusFiniteOrder.lean#L150-L169","doc":"**The substrate form, over `ℂ`** — which is where the branch locus actually lives,\nsince the six roots of the branch sextic `D` are complex. A real `l > 1`, viewed in `ℂ`,\nstabilises no nonempty finite set of nonzero complex numbers.\n\nApplied to `B = {D = 0}`: `D(0) = 16 ≠ 0` so `0 ∉ B`, `B` is nonempty, and `l = q₀² > 1`.\nSo **no dilation by the substrate shift can permute the branch locus** — which is the\nconclusion #6769 and #7185 reach through the prose step, now machine-checked from\n\"permutes `B`\" onward. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.BranchLocusFiniteOrder.not_stabilizes_of_one_lt_real","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.BranchLocusFiniteOrder.not_stabilizes_of_one_lt_real","kind":"theorem","line":139,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/BranchLocusFiniteOrder.lean#L139-L148","doc":"**The substrate form, over `ℝ`.** A real scaling `l > 1` stabilises no nonempty\nfinite set of nonzero reals. The W1 shift is `l = q₀²` with `q₀ ≈ 1.10998 > 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.BranchLocusFiniteOrder.one_lt_pow_of_one_lt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.BranchLocusFiniteOrder.one_lt_pow_of_one_lt","kind":"theorem","line":127,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/BranchLocusFiniteOrder.lean#L127-L137","doc":"A real `l > 1` has no finite multiplicative order: `l^n > 1` for every `n ≥ 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.BranchLocusFiniteOrder.exists_pow_eq_one_of_stabilizes","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.BranchLocusFiniteOrder.exists_pow_eq_one_of_stabilizes","kind":"theorem","line":84,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/BranchLocusFiniteOrder.lean#L84-L125","doc":"**The pigeonhole step.** If a finite set `S` of a field contains no zero and is\nmapped into itself by multiplication by `l`, then `l` has finite multiplicative order,\nbounded by `S.card`.\n\nThis is the half of the W1 tranches' prose step that is not geometric. The `S.card + 1`\norbit points `l^0·r, …, l^{S.card}·r` all lie in `S`, which has `S.card` elements, so two\ncoincide; the common factor `l^i·r` is nonzero, so cancelling it gives `l^n = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.BranchLocusFiniteOrder.pow_smul_mem","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.BranchLocusFiniteOrder.pow_smul_mem","kind":"theorem","line":72,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/BranchLocusFiniteOrder.lean#L72-L82","doc":"Every `l`-orbit point of a member of `S` stays in `S`, by induction on the exponent.\nOnly `maps into` is assumed — not that `r ↦ l·r` is a bijection of `S`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}